cards.body:
In April 2026 OJK summoned the online lender PT Indosaku Digital Teknologi and its industry association after a reported incident involving a debt collector in Semarang (press release SP 84/DKPU/OJK/IV/2026). OJK stressed that every provider is responsible for the third parties it appoints to collect, and cited POJK 22/2023.

After a special examination, OJK announced on 8 May 2026 (SP 94/DKPU/OJK/V/2026) that it had found non-compliance in managing and supervising collection, especially by third parties. Sanctions:

- an administrative **fine of Rp875 million**;
- a **written warning to the president director**;
- an **order to carry out a remediation plan**: better collection policies, stronger contracts with collection agencies covering conduct, oversight and sanctions, quality control, and **training, monitoring and evaluation of collectors**, including complaint handling.

Indosaku is a fintech lender, not a bank, but the same POJK 22/2023 collection rules bind Bank Arunika.

OJK and the members of the Task Force for Eradicating Illegal Financial Activities (Satgas PASTI), supported by banking and payment industry associations, set up the **Indonesia Anti-Scam Centre (IASC)**, which began operating on **22 November 2024**.

OJK's November 2026 press release (SP 219/OJK/DKPU/XI/2026) reported that by 28 October 2026 the IASC had:

- received about **738,000 reports**, about 347,000 of them lodged by victims **through banks and payment providers** and the rest directly;
- had about **1.47 million accounts reported** and about **744,000 accounts blocked**;
- **blocked about Rp968 billion** of victims' funds and **returned about Rp209 billion** to victims from accounts at 24 banks.

**Lesson:** front-line staff are part of the system. Fast logging and clear customer education decide whether money can be stopped.
